\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

The experiments in \S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}--\ref{sec:consensus_validation} reveal that lead-lag conclusions in iterative training collapse are fragile along multiple axes.
This section distills three methodological pitfalls (\S\ref{sec:pitfalls}), proposes a multi-method consensus framework to quantify their impact and identify the most reliable individual method (\S\ref{sec:consensus}), and calibrates the framework's error rates using built-in experimental controls (\S\ref{sec:calibration}).


\subsection{Methodological Pitfalls in Lead-Lag Analysis}
\label{sec:pitfalls}

We identify three pitfalls that, individually or in combination, can produce incorrect conclusions about temporal precedence in iterative training systems.
Each is documented below with representative data from our experiments; full results appear in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.

\paragraph{Pitfall 1: Method sensitivity.}
Applied to the same data ($\alpha{=}1.0$, 4~seeds, 16 canary$\to$downstream pairs: 4 candidate canaries $\times$ 4 downstream metrics, including candidates later excluded per \S\ref{sec:case_study}), five lead-lag detection methods yield FDR significance rates ranging from 100\% (naive cross-correlation) to 37.5\% (permutation Granger), with Toda--Yamamoto achieving moderate rates (3--4/8 at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$); inter-method agreement is Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.185$, indicating poor agreement (\S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}).
The spurious-signal rate (the fraction of pairs flagged by the most liberal method but not confirmed by the most conservative) is at least 62.5\% across the primary methods.
This pitfall is dangerous because method choice alone can determine whether a lead-lag relationship is deemed present or absent.
In a literature where single-method analyses are the norm, the implicit assumption that method choice does not matter is demonstrably false.

\paragraph{Pitfall 2: Spurious regression under shared trends.}
A single preprocessing decision, first-differencing to remove shared non-stationary trends~\cite{granger1974spurious}, can eliminate an entire analysis channel.
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, canary$\to$perplexity pairs appear near-universally significant in undifferenced analysis ($F{=}16$--$36$) but yield $F{\approx}0$, $p{=}0.99$ after differencing (Figure~\ref{fig:differencing_dmax}a; \S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}).
In contrast, canary$\to$diversity pairs retain unanimous FDR significance after differencing (24/24$\to$24/24).
Without the differencing diagnostic, an analyst would conclude that canaries predict perplexity, a conclusion entirely driven by co-trending rather than temporal precedence.
The danger lies in the selective nature of this confound: only by applying the diagnostic can one determine which channels are genuine and which are artifacts of shared non-stationarity.

\paragraph{Pitfall 3: Regime dependence.}
Even after fixing the statistical method, the contamination level~$\alpha$ determines whether a lead-lag relationship is detectable.
Permutation Granger tests (first-differenced, BH FDR-corrected at $q{=}0.05$) find 6/8 pairs FDR-significant at both $\alpha{=}0.50$ (2~fp32 seeds, consistent) and $\alpha{=}1.0$, but 0/8 through $\alpha{=}0.45$ (\S\ref{sec:regime}).
The transition zone at $\alpha{\approx}0.45$--$0.50$ is where method divergence is most dangerous: correlation-based methods find significance across all levels while causal methods remain null through $\alpha{=}0.45$ (a faint single-pair signal appears at $\alpha{=}0.25$, FDR $p{=}0.053$, but does not reach aggregate detection), creating the maximum risk of spurious conclusions when only a single (liberal) method is used.
This regime dependence is consistent with theoretical expectations about signal-to-noise limits in short ecological time series \cite{boettiger2012limits}, and suggests that detectability boundaries should be explicitly mapped rather than assumed.


\subsection{Multi-Method Consensus Framework}
\label{sec:consensus}

The pitfalls above motivate a multi-method consensus approach inspired by specification curve analysis \cite{simonsohn2020specification} and multi-method validation in early-warning signal detection \cite{scheffer2009early}: rather than relying on any single method, we apply multiple independent methods to quantify how analytic choices determine conclusions, using agreement thresholds as diagnostic benchmarks.

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
Given $M$ lead-lag detection methods $\{m_1, \ldots, m_M\}$ and $N$ independent random seeds, a canary$\to$downstream pair $(X, Y)$ is classified as \textsc{robust} if and only if:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item At least $K$ of $M$ methods judge the pair FDR-significant, where each method independently applies Benjamini--Hochberg correction at $q{=}0.05$; and
    \item At least $S$ of $N$ seeds replicate this conclusion.
\end{enumerate}
The thresholds $(K, S)$ control the trade-off between false positive and false negative rates.
Methods with different inductive biases (correlation-based methods that detect any temporal association versus Granger-type methods that test directional predictive improvement \cite{granger1969investigating}) provide complementary evidence.
Their convergence on a given pair constitutes stronger evidence than agreement among methods sharing the same statistical assumptions.
We note that Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not true causality \cite{shojaie2022granger}; our framework inherits this limitation.

\paragraph{Method tiering.}
The five methods are organized into two tiers based on empirical reliability.
Three \emph{primary} methods---naive cross-correlation, differenced cross-correlation, and permutation Granger causality---produce consistent results across independent analysis pipelines and exhibit adequate statistical power at $T{=}11$ generations.
Two methods are classified as \emph{supplementary}: threshold onset detection due to power limitations at $T{=}11$ under corrected $3\sigma$ calibration (\S\ref{sec:threshold_limitation}), and Toda--Yamamoto Granger causality, which achieves moderate detection (3--4/8 at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$) but with reduced power relative to the primary methods (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}).
The consensus panel thus comprises four contributing methods (three primary, one moderate supplementary), with threshold onset near-inoperative at $T{=}11$.
The consensus threshold $K{\geq}3$ out of 5 methods requires agreement among at least the three primary methods or two primary methods plus TY, making $K{\geq}3$ a \emph{stronger} standard than a simple majority vote would suggest.

\paragraph{Sequential filtering pipeline.}
In practice, the $K$-of-$M$ voting operates over all five methods applied independently to each pair; the sequential pipeline below describes the order in which results are examined, enabling early termination and interpretable diagnostics at each stage (Figure~\ref{fig:consensus_workflow}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Naive correlation scan.} Cross-correlation at maximum lag~5 with BH correction serves as a permissive baseline; all pairs with any temporal association pass.
    \item \textbf{First-differencing diagnostic.} Cross-correlation is recomputed on first-differenced series, testing whether temporal associations survive removal of shared linear trends. Pairs that lose significance are flagged as potential spurious regressions.
    \item \textbf{Permutation Granger causality.} Permutation-based Granger causality tests \cite{shojaie2022granger} on first-differenced series (5{,}000 permutations, BH correction at $q{=}0.05$) assess directional predictive improvement: whether past values of $X$ improve prediction of $Y$ beyond $Y$'s own history.
    \item \textbf{Multi-seed replication.} The conclusion must hold across $S$ of $N$ independent training seeds, filtering seed-specific artifacts.
\end{enumerate}
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, this pipeline progressively filters: 16 pairs pass Stage~1, 14 survive Stage~2 (differencing removes 2 marginal canary$\to$perplexity pairs), 6 survive Stage~3 (Granger testing eliminates remaining canary$\to$perplexity pairs and failed canary candidates), and all~6 survive Stage~4 (multi-seed replication confirms all Granger-significant pairs).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/consensus_workflow.pdf}
\caption{Sequential filtering pipeline of the multi-method consensus framework. Starting from all candidate canary$\to$downstream pairs, the pipeline applies four stages: (1)~naive cross-correlation scan (permissive baseline), (2)~first-differencing diagnostic (removes shared-trend artifacts), (3)~permutation Granger causality (tests directional predictive improvement), and (4)~multi-seed replication (filters seed-specific artifacts). At $\alpha{=}1.0$ (Pythia-410M), the pipeline reduces 16 candidate pairs to 6 robust pairs: 16$\to$14$\to$6$\to$6. The $K$-of-$M$ consensus vote operates in parallel across all five methods; the sequential ordering provides interpretable diagnostics at each stage.}
\label{fig:consensus_workflow}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Practical guidance.}
The consensus thresholds $(K, S)$ should be chosen to match the analyst's tolerance for false positives versus false negatives.
Table~\ref{tab:practical_guidance} provides four recommended tiers, calibrated against our experimental controls (\S\ref{sec:calibration}).
Tier~3 ($K{\geq}4/5$, $S{\geq}3/4$) achieves the best trade-off under the full 5-method panel: zero false positives while recovering all six reference pairs; however, threshold onset's near-zero power at $3\sigma$ (\S\ref{sec:threshold_limitation}) makes it near-inoperative, and Toda--Yamamoto provides moderate supplementary signal (3--4/8 at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$; \S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}), making $K{\geq}3$ the operationally relevant consensus threshold.
With four contributing methods (three primary plus TY), $K{\geq}3$ draws on a broader evidence base than the three primary methods alone; TY's moderate detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$ strengthens consensus conclusions where it contributes a positive vote.
Cross-architecture validation confirms $K{\geq}3$ reaches ${\geq}6/8$ in 10 of 14 architecture-seed combinations (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Method subset ablation confirms that this threshold is uniquely optimal: at $K{=}3/5$, the null-control FPR is 37.5\% (unacceptable); at $K{=}4/5$, FPR drops to 0\% while retaining 6/6 reference-pair sensitivity; at $K{=}5/5$, FPR remains 0\% but sensitivity falls to 3/6.
Shapley value analysis (exact enumeration of all 32 coalitions; Figure~\ref{fig:shapley}) identifies naive cross-correlation as the sole positive contributor to FPR (Shapley(FPR)${=}{+}0.348$) and permutation Granger as the strongest FPR suppressor (Shapley(FPR)${=}{-}0.194$), the only method combining 0\% null-control FPR with high sensitivity.
Toda--Yamamoto's Shapley contribution (FNR${=}{-}0.158$) reflects moderate supplementary value: it reduces consensus FNR without introducing false positives (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/shapley_method_contribution.pdf}
\caption{Shapley value decomposition of method contributions to consensus FPR and FNR (exact enumeration of all $2^5{=}32$ coalitions, $K{=}\max(1,|S|{-}1)$). FPR is computed at $\alpha{=}0.0$ (3~seeds $\times$ 8~pairs${=}$24 observations); FNR at $\alpha{=}1.0$ (4~seeds, 6 reference pairs). Naive cross-correlation is the only positive FPR contributor (${+}0.348$); permutation Granger is the strongest FPR suppressor (${-}0.194$). Toda--Yamamoto contributes moderate FNR suppression (Shapley FNR${=}{-}0.158$) as a supplementary method (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}).}
\label{fig:shapley}
\end{figure}
Tier~4 requires unanimity across methods and seeds, which incurs elevated false negative rates; TY's moderate detection (3--4/8) means that requiring $5/5$ unanimity is substantially more restrictive than $4/5$, particularly at intermediate contamination levels where TY may not contribute a positive vote.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Practical guidance tiers for lead-lag consensus analysis. Tier~3 ($K{\geq}4/5$, $S{\geq}3/4$) is uniquely optimal under the full 5-method panel; with threshold onset power-limited (\S\ref{sec:threshold_limitation}) and Toda--Yamamoto providing moderate supplementary signal (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}), $K{\geq}3$ draws on four contributing methods. FPR is calibrated at $\alpha{=}0.0$ (null control, $M{=}5$ methods, $N{=}3$ seeds, Pythia-410M). FNR is calibrated at $\alpha{=}1.0$ against 6 effect-size reference pairs using a 4-method panel (excluding differenced cross-correlation); $N{=}4$ seeds (\S\ref{sec:calibration}).}
\label{tab:practical_guidance}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Tier & Use Case & $K/M$ & $S/N$ & FPR & FNR \\
\midrule
1 & Exploratory     & ${\geq}1/5$ & ${\geq}1/N$ & 100\%  & 0\%    \\
2 & Screening       & ${\geq}3/5$ & ${\geq}2/N$ & 37.5\% & 0\%    \\
3 & Confirmatory    & ${\geq}4/5$ & ${\geq}3/N$ & 0\%    & 0\%    \\
4 & Conservative    & $5/5$       & $N/N$        & 0\%    & 50\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Empirical Calibration}
\label{sec:calibration}

We calibrate the framework using two built-in experimental controls.
Full numerical results are reported in Table~\ref{tab:fpr_fnr}.

\paragraph{False positive rate.}
The $\alpha{=}0.0$ condition serves as a null control: no synthetic data is injected, so any detected lead-lag association is a false positive.
At $K{=}1$ (any single method suffices), FPR${=}$75\%: naive cross-correlation flags 6/8 forward-direction pairs.
At $K{=}2$, FPR drops to ${\leq}$37.5\%; at $K{=}3$, to ${\leq}$12.5\%; and at $K{\geq}4$, FPR${=}$0\%.
The consensus framework at Tier~3 matches the false positive performance of the best individual method (permutation Granger, 0\%) while providing robustness against miscalibration of any single method.

\paragraph{False negative rate.}
At $\alpha{=}1.0$ with 4~seeds, we define six \emph{effect-size reference pairs} using criteria independent of the consensus methods:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] The canary$\to$downstream drift ratio at $\alpha{=}1.0$ must exceed $3\times$ the $\alpha{=}0.0$ baseline drift (canary ratios: entropy $11.93\times$, ECE $73.12\times$, both far above threshold); and
    \item[(b)] Canary onset must strictly precede downstream onset by at least one generation (canary onset: generation~1; diversity onset: generations~3--9).
\end{enumerate}
These criteria yield token entropy and ECE each predicting distinct-1, distinct-2, and distinct-3~\cite{li2016diversity}.
The perplexity channel is excluded because its onset (generation~1) coincides with canary onset, consistent with, but not derived from, the differencing diagnostic finding that canary$\to$perplexity associations are spurious (\S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}).
The effect-size threshold is robust: at $2\times$, $3\times$, and $5\times$ the baseline drift, the reference set remains identical (6~pairs), because canary ratios ($11.93\times$ and $73.12\times$) far exceed all thresholds.

FNR is calibrated using a 4-method panel excluding differenced cross-correlation, which shares statistical properties with detrending-based criteria and could introduce circularity.
At $K{\geq}3/4$, $S{\geq}3/4$, all six reference pairs are recovered (FNR${=}$0\%).
At $K{=}4/4$, $S{=}4/4$, FNR rises to 50\%, reflecting TY's moderate detection power (4/8 at $\alpha{=}1.0$; \S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}).

\paragraph{Symmetric calibration.}
Both FPR and FNR are validated on the joint method${\times}$seed dimension: FPR across 3~seeds at $\alpha{=}0.0$ (0/24 pairs at $K{\geq}3$, $3\sigma$) and FNR across 4~seeds at $\alpha{=}1.0$ (6/6 reference pairs recovered at $K{\geq}3/4$, $S{\geq}3/4$).
Cross-seed FPR variance is negligible: all three null-control seeds yield 0/8 robust pairs at $K{\geq}3$ ($3\sigma$), confirming seed-independent performance.


\paragraph{Threshold onset detection: power limitations at $3\sigma$.}
\label{sec:threshold_limitation}
Post-hoc validation of the threshold onset implementation (M028) revealed that the published $2\sigma$ calibration was mislabeled as $3\sigma$.
Under corrected $3\sigma$ calibration, threshold onset yields 0/8 significant pairs in 8 of 12 cross-architecture conditions (Table~\ref{tab:generalization}) and at most 2/8 in the remainder, reducing it to a near-zero-power method at $T{=}11$ generations; any threshold onset detections should therefore be interpreted as lower-bound estimates.
This low power is expected: with only 10 generation-over-generation differences, the null variance estimate has few degrees of freedom, and a $3\sigma$ exceedance threshold becomes overly conservative.
We retain threshold onset as a supplementary method for completeness and report its results transparently, but adopt permutation Granger forward significance as the primary detection indicator in cross-architecture analyses.

\paragraph{Toda--Yamamoto Granger: moderate supplementary signal.}
\label{sec:ty_limitation}
Post-hoc analysis identified a statsmodels API incompatibility that initially produced artificially low Toda--Yamamoto detection rates.
After correcting the implementation, TY achieves moderate detection: 3/8 forward-direction pairs at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (seed~42), 3/8 at $\alpha{=}0.75$, and 4/8 at $\alpha{=}1.0$ (Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison}).
These rates are lower than permutation Granger (6--8/8) but confirm that TY captures genuine signal at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$, consistent with the method's theoretical motivation~\cite{toda1995statistical}.
TY is classified as a moderate supplementary method: it contributes to the consensus panel but does not serve as a primary detection indicator due to reduced statistical power at $T{=}11$ (the augmentation order $d_{\max}$ consumes additional degrees of freedom) and sensitivity to the $d_{\max}$ hyperparameter (Figure~\ref{fig:differencing_dmax}b).
With TY providing moderate signal, the consensus panel comprises four contributing methods (three primary plus TY as supplementary), strengthening the $K{\geq}3$ threshold: at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (seed~42), $K{\geq}3$ rises from 5/8 to 6/8 with TY's corrected contribution.
At $\alpha{\geq}0.50$, $K{\geq}4$ consensus (4 of 5 methods agree) emerges in 3/8 pairs, demonstrating that multi-method agreement extends beyond the primary trio.

\subsection{Multi-Method Consensus Test (MMCT)}
\label{sec:mmct}

The uniform threshold ($K{\geq}4/5$) achieves FPR${=}$0\% and FNR${=}$0\% on reference extremes (\S\ref{sec:calibration}), but this ad-hoc threshold lacks formal statistical grounding: null-calibrated permutation testing assigns $p{=}0.16$ to $K{\geq}4/5$, failing to reach significance at conventional levels.
The threshold also treats all methods equally, despite the heterogeneous error profiles documented in \S\ref{sec:pitfalls}: naive cross-correlation has 75\% forward-direction null-control FPR while permutation Granger has 0\%.
We formalize the \emph{Multi-Method Consensus Test} (MMCT), a weighted consensus framework that assigns each method influence proportional to its empirical specificity, following the specification curve tradition~\cite{simonsohn2020specification} in which analytical choices are systematically varied and aggregated rather than arbitrarily fixed~\cite{bell2022modeling}.
MMCT serves as an evaluation framework rather than an end in itself: its primary function is to enable systematic comparison of detection methods under controlled conditions, identifying which individual methods are reliable and why others fail.
We do not claim a novel statistical method; MMCT is a consensus testing protocol adapted to the specific constraints of iterative training analysis: short series ($T{=}5$--$20$), non-stationarity by design, and heterogeneous method sensitivities.

\paragraph{FPR-inverse weighting.}
Let $\hat{\text{FPR}}_k$ denote the empirical false positive rate of method $m_k$, estimated from $N_0$ null-control observations at $\alpha{=}0.0$ across $S$ seeds.
Each method receives weight
\begin{equation}
    w_k = \frac{1}{\max(\hat{\text{FPR}}_k,\; \epsilon)},
    \label{eq:fpr_weight}
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon{=}0.01$ prevents division by zero.
Methods with low FPR (e.g., permutation Granger at 0\%, receiving $w_k{=}100$) dominate the consensus; methods with high FPR (e.g., naive cross-correlation at 75\%, receiving $w_k{\approx}1.3$) are effectively downweighted.

\paragraph{Consensus score.}
Given $M$ methods that return binary decisions $d_k \in \{0, 1\}$ (FDR-significant or not) for a candidate pair $(X, Y)$, the weighted consensus score is:
\begin{equation}
    W(X \to Y) = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{M} w_k \cdot d_k}{\sum_{k=1}^{M} w_k}.
    \label{eq:consensus_score}
\end{equation}
When all weights equal~1 (uniform mode), $W$ reduces to $K/M$, and thresholding at $W \geq 4/5$ recovers the uniform $K{\geq}4$ rule exactly.

\paragraph{Statistical testing.}
The null distribution of $W$ is estimated from the $\alpha{=}0.0$ control condition, where no synthetic contamination is present and any detected lead-lag association is a false positive.
The $N_0$ null-control pairs yield a reference distribution $\{W_0^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{N_0}$, and the MMCT $p$-value is:
\begin{equation}
    p_{\text{MMCT}} = \frac{|\{j : W_0^{(j)} \geq W_{\text{obs}}\}|}{N_0}.
    \label{eq:pvalue}
\end{equation}
We reject the null of no lead-lag relationship at significance level $\alpha_{\text{test}}$.

\paragraph{Bidirectional filtering.}
MMCT tests both directions independently: $W(X \to Y)$ and $W(Y \to X)$.
A stricter bidirectional mode requires forward significance with reverse non-significance.
Empirically, bidirectional and forward-only detection rates are identical across all contamination levels tested, indicating that pairs reaching weighted consensus in the forward direction consistently fail to reach consensus in reverse.
We therefore adopt forward-only (weighted pair forward) as the default MMCT configuration.
Algorithm~\ref{alg:mmct} summarizes the procedure.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Multi-Method Consensus Test (MMCT)}
\label{alg:mmct}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Methods $\{m_1, \ldots, m_M\}$; pair $(X, Y)$; null pairs $\{(X_0^{(j)}, Y_0^{(j)})\}_{j=1}^{N_0}$; floor $\epsilon$; level $\alpha_{\text{test}}$
\Ensure Decision $d$, score $W$, $p$-value
\Statex \textbf{Phase 1: Estimate weights from null control}
\For{$k = 1, \ldots, M$}
    \State $\hat{\mathrm{FPR}}_k \gets$ null-control rejection rate of $m_k$
    \State $w_k \gets 1 / \max(\hat{\mathrm{FPR}}_k, \epsilon)$
\EndFor
\Statex \textbf{Phase 2: Compute weighted consensus}
\For{$k = 1, \ldots, M$}
    \State $d_k \gets m_k(X, Y)$ \Comment{1 if FDR-significant}
\EndFor
\State $W \gets \sum_k w_k d_k \;/\; \sum_k w_k$
\Statex \textbf{Phase 3: Null-distribution $p$-value}
\For{$j = 1, \ldots, N_0$}
    \State $W_0^{(j)} \gets$ consensus score of $(X_0^{(j)}, Y_0^{(j)})$
\EndFor
\State $p \gets |\{j : W_0^{(j)} \geq W\}| / N_0$
\State \Return $\mathbb{1}[p < \alpha_{\text{test}}]$, $W$, $p$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Empirical comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:mmct_ablation} compares per-method detection rates across contamination levels, revealing the systematic comparison that motivates our method recommendation.
The five methods exhibit sharply divergent dose-response profiles: naive cross-correlation maintains elevated FPR at the null (75\%) but rises to 87.5\% at $\alpha{=}0.75$; differenced cross-correlation achieves zero null-control FPR but detects in both forward and reverse directions, failing to provide directional separation; threshold onset detects only at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (50\%) and returns to zero at $\alpha{=}0.75$ under $3\sigma$ calibration; Toda--Yamamoto achieves moderate detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$ (37.5\%) with zero null-control FPR but lower sensitivity than permutation Granger.
Permutation Granger alone provides the cleanest dose-response profile, with a sharp step function from 0/8 (blind spot, $\alpha{\leq}0.45$) to 6/8 (onset, $\alpha{=}0.50$, dual-seed confirmed) and reliable detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.75$ (Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison}; \S\ref{sec:regime}).

Permutation Granger produces binary per-pair verdicts that yield a sharp phase transition in detection: a faint signal at $\alpha{=}0.25$ (best pair FDR $p{=}0.053$) that does not reach aggregate detection through $\alpha{=}0.45$, then an abrupt jump to 6/8 at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (fp32), with a non-monotonic dip at $\alpha{=}0.60$ (s42: 3/8, s43: 0/8; dual-seed confirmed synchronous collapse zone; \S\ref{sec:regime}).
This step-function profile, combined with zero null-control FPR and Shapley FPR suppression of $-0.194$, identifies PermG as the recommended individual detection method.
Cross-architecture validation confirms PermG fwd ${\geq}6/8$ in 12 of 14 conditions, with multi-method consensus ($K{\geq}3$) reaching ${\geq}6/8$ in 10 of 14 (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
MMCT weighted consensus further captures graded detection strength even where individual methods remain below threshold, providing formal $p$-values that the uniform $K$-of-$M$ threshold lacks; however, the systematic comparison reveals that PermG alone provides comparable or superior detection to the full consensus framework at all contamination levels tested.
With TY contributing as a fourth method, full 5/5 consensus becomes achievable: at $\alpha{=}0.50$ (seed~42), the ECE$\to$distinct-3 pair reaches significance across all five methods, demonstrating that the consensus panel can converge unanimously even near the onset boundary when the canary--downstream coupling is strong.

